## Why This Is Asked
Tests offline-first design, deterministic scoring, and conflict handling in sync.

## Key Concepts
- Offline-first workflows and local persistence
- Deterministic scoring with seeds for reproducibility
- Conflict resolution strategies (last-write-wins, vector clocks)

## Code Example
```javascript
function scoreBaseline(params, seed){
  let x = seed ^ (params.user_id_masked || '').length;
  x = (x * 1664525) >>> 0;
  const localeFactor = (params.locale || 'en').charCodeAt(0) & 0xff;
  x = (x ^ localeFactor) >>> 0;
  const versionFactor = (params.local_version || 'v1').length;
  x = (x * (versionFactor + 7)) >>> 0;
  const score = Math.abs(x) % 100;
  return score;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
This angle tests practical edge compute handling: budget-aware routing, deterministic fallbacks, and testability in real apps.

## Key Concepts
- Budget-aware orchestration: enforce per-request time budgets for compute paths
- Deterministic fallback: seed heuristic with user_id_masked and client_timestamp for reproducibility
- Telemetry: track latency, fallback usage, and model_version for auditing
- Testability: budget-bound tests, fairness across devices, end-to-end reproducibility

## Code Example
```javascript
function selectScore(input){
  const {budgetMs, start}=input;
  const canRunModel = simulateModelCost(input) <= budgetMs;
  if(!canRunModel){
    return heuristicScore(input.user_id_masked, input.client_timestamp);
  }
  return modelScore(input);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you ensure reproducibility if the heuristic depends on timestamps with clock drift?
- How would you expand this to multi-tenant environments with fair queueing?

## Why This Is Asked
Ensures robust observability, tenant isolation, and safe release discipline for production ML services.

## Key Concepts
- Per-tenant drift detection across model_versions
- Canary rollout and rollback criteria
- Data residency and privacy considerations
- Drift metrics schema and reporting
- Synthetic drift testing and alerting

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo drift score computation between old and new distributions
function computeDrift(oldDist, newDist){ 
  let drift = 0;
  for (let i = 0; i < oldDist.length; i++){
    let p = oldDist[i] || 1e-9;
    let q = newDist[i] || 1e-9;
    drift += p * Math.log(p / q);
  }
  return drift;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle a tenant with drift but small user base?
- How would you scale drift computation to thousands of tenants?

## Why This Is Asked
This question evaluates understanding of consent-driven data minimization, offline safety, and the relationship between consent lifecycle and CCA score calculation.

## Key Concepts
- Consent tokens gate data exposure
- policy_version controls exposure granularity
- Idempotent, offline-safe compute operations
- Per-user data retention settings

## Code Example
```javascript
async function computeCCA(userIdMasked, consentToken, policyVersion) {
  const consent = await consentStore.validate(consentToken, policyVersion);
  if (!consent?.allowed) {
    throw new Error('Not authorized');
  }
  const score = await calculateScore(userIdMasked, consent);
  return { user_id_masked: userIdMasked, cca_score: score };
}
```

